{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner decisions on the two landed design notes, answering 39402319, ee24625e, ee87edcb, 1f8fecbc and ca2b6b35.\n\n**Retiring a founder (273cbd75).**\n1. The operator key reads a new-pin register's log with no window. I accept the rule with the checker's point in ca2b6b35: a thief with a stolen operator key reads that log with no trace until the register is replaced. The note's sentence \"gives it no new knowledge\" stays only with that stated beside it. Add that at the next edit.\n2. No refusal list at the Worker, as the note recommends.\n3. Do not build this now. It stays a design until a slice needs it. So nothing holds on my ruling.\n\n**The four facts in 1f8fecbc.** The checker confirmed them in c978dc5e. File them as your own items. Prove the second one first with a run: a claim with three failed attempts stays pending and counts toward `max`, so 64 such claims refuse every later `found`. If it runs as you read it, a host outage can close a register, and that one takes priority over the other three.\n\n**separate-recovery (991129fc).** No opt-in now. No deployment adds its pins, and default and newest stay. The command line and the page always make a separate key, so no real room reaches the shape it refuses, and its limits cost more than that gain.\n\n**A Git read token (970c701c).**\n1. Do not build the one-hour pin before real calls 2 and 3 run. I run the calls.\n2. `revoke-reads` follows that change and is not built now.\n3. No notice to a lane, as the note and the checker recommend. Build 9beb1933 on the destination alone, as you are.\n4. Order: one next cohort at a time. When a slice builds the token pin, it carries separate-recovery with it, so rooms do not pick among partial cohorts. The founder pin joins only if a slice needs it then.\n\nAdd Rita's step to \"Respond to a stolen key\" now. Say whether it has run, from my result on call 1. Also correct the wrong reason in `notes/implementation/2026-11-01-signed-read-refusal.md`